-- title:
--   The spaces H_k(Ω) and H^{α/2}(Ω)
-- statement:
--   **The two function spaces of Bux–Kassmann–Schulze's Section 1**, for a set $\Omega$ and a
--   kernel $k$:
--
--   $$H_k(\Omega) = \bigl\{f \in L^2(\Omega) \;:\; \lvert f\rvert_{H_k(\Omega)} < \infty\bigr\},
--   \qquad
--   H^{\alpha/2}(\Omega) = \bigl\{f \in L^2(\Omega) \;:\; \lvert f\rvert_{H^{\alpha/2}(\Omega)} < \infty\bigr\},$$
--
--   where $\lvert f\rvert^2_{H_k(\Omega)} = \iint_{\Omega\times\Omega} (f(y)-f(x))^2 k(x,y)$ and
--   $\lvert f\rvert^2_{H^{\alpha/2}(\Omega)}$ is the same with $k$ replaced by the jump kernel
--   $\lVert x-y\rVert^{-d-\alpha}$.
--
--   Both seminorms are lower Lebesgue integrals valued in $[0,\infty]$, so they are defined without
--   assuming the integrand measurable; membership asks that the value be finite, written here as
--   `≠ ⊤`. Square-integrability is `MemLp f 2` for the measure restricted to $\Omega$. The source
--   also endows each space with a norm combining the seminorm with $\lVert f\rVert_{L^2(\Omega)}$;
--   only the sets are needed for the inclusion (5) and for the identity Theorem 1.4 asserts, so only
--   the sets are defined here.
--
--   Whether these two sets coincide is the whole subject of the paper: equation (5)
--   (`QFS.Hs_subset_Hk`) gives one inclusion cheaply from the upper bound of assumption (2), and the
--   reverse is Theorem 1.4, resting on Theorem 1.1.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/cc13121ff7b667aa31d9a3cd85088f5575d56ca5/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Section1.lean#L94-L103

